\begin{cases}
\sqrt{x^2+y^2} + y-1 = 3 \\
\sqrt{x^2+y^2} - y+1 = 3
\end{cases}
\] Simplifying the first equation, we get \[y = 1 - \sqrt{9-x^2}\] which is the equation of a parabola with vertex at (0,0). Simplifying the second equation, we get \[y = \sqrt{9-x^2} + 1\] which is the equation of another parabola with vertex at (0,1). </think> <answer> 1 </answer>
